{#
    The Odd Badge base template.

    The five stylesheets are the design system's own, copied verbatim into `public/` and linked
    with `asset()`: no build step, because the system ships plain CSS and a bundler would add a
    decision nobody needs and a failure mode in `symfony serve`. The order matters, and it is the
    order the design system's README gives: fonts, then the three token files, then the component
    layer that spends them.

    The theme is an attribute on `<html>`: absent means follow the operating system, and
    `data-theme="light"` or `data-theme="dark"` pins it. Absent is what this template emits,
    because no card asks for a switcher and a switcher invented here would be a product decision
    taken in a foundation commit.

    `lang` is read off the framework rather than passed in. ADR 007 resolves the locale from
    `Accept-Language` through `framework.set_locale_from_accept_language`, negotiated down to
    `default_locale` rather than answering 400, and nothing anywhere takes a locale as a
    parameter. `app.locale` is the `LocaleSwitcher`'s answer, which is the request's locale
    during a request and `default_locale` everywhere else; `app.request.locale` would be the
    same value with a null to trip over the moment something renders outside one.

    There is deliberately no navigation, no footer and no flash region here. Each of those
    belongs to a card.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ app.locale }}">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
        <title>{% block title %}Odd Badge{% endblock %}</title>

        {% block stylesheets %}
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('fonts/fonts.css') }}">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('tokens/colors.css') }}">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('tokens/typography.css') }}">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('tokens/spacing.css') }}">
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('styles.css') }}">
        {% endblock %}

        {% block javascripts %}
        {% endblock %}

        {#
            The one hook a page has into `<head>`, and it is empty by default because everything
            above it is the same for every page in the tree.

            ADR 009 rule 15 is what asked for it: a `/me/...` page is a perspective on an explicit
            `/members/{principal}/...` URL rather than a second resource, so it carries
            `<link rel="canonical">` to the explicit one, and `/` will owe the same element when it
            forwards to the discovery feed. A canonical link only counts in `<head>`, so a page with
            nowhere to put one cannot obey the rule.

            It is deliberately not called `meta` or `seo`. It is the place a page adds to the head,
            and the next thing that belongs here is whatever the next card needs rather than
            whatever this one happened to need first.
        #}
        {% block head %}{% endblock %}
    </head>
    <body>
        {% block body %}{% endblock %}
    </body>
</html>
